WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.420
November 15

2
00:00:02.421 --> 00:00:05.945
Dear Daddy Longlegs,

3
00:00:05.946 --> 00:00:08.702
I've changed my name!

4
00:00:08.703 --> 00:00:12.032
I'm still Jerusha to the college.

5
00:00:12.033 --> 00:00:15.319
But I'm Judy to everyone else.

6
00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.982
Usually somebody else comes up with a nickname.

7
00:00:17.983 --> 00:00:22.024
(Just like I gave you the name Daddy Longlegs!)

8
00:00:22.025 --> 00:00:26.864
So I guess it's sad that I had to make one up for myself.

9
00:00:26.865 --> 00:00:29.981
But that's not totally true.

10
00:00:29.982 --> 00:00:35.033
One of the little orphans used to call me Judy

11
00:00:35.034 --> 00:00:39.267
. He couldn't say Jerusha when he was learning to

12
00:00:39.268 --> 00:00:42.631
talk. I always wondered where my name came

13
00:00:42.632 --> 00:00:47.086
from. One day, when I still lived at the orphanage, I asked Mrs.

14
00:00:47.087 --> 00:00:51.085
Lippett. "Did my parents name me Jerusha

15
00:00:51.086 --> 00:00:53.048
Abbott?" Mrs. Lippett

16
00:00:53.049 --> 00:00:57.029
frowned. "Oh no. You were like many of the other orphans

17
00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:00.665
here. We didn't know your parents' names or your

18
00:01:00.666 --> 00:01:07.039
name. Someone just left you on the orphanage

19
00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:10.837
steps." "Then where did my name come

20
00:01:10.838 --> 00:01:15.462
from?" "The same place I get all the orphans' names—the telephone

21
00:01:15.463 --> 00:01:18.764
book!" Mrs. Lippett sounded proud of

22
00:01:18.765 --> 00:01:23.907
herself. "You'll find Abbott right there on the first

23
00:01:23.908 --> 00:01:27.261
page." "But where did you find

24
00:01:27.262 --> 00:01:32.392
Jerusha?" "On a tombstone in the

25
00:01:32.393 --> 00:01:36.516
cemetery! Now get back to work, Jerusha, and stop asking

26
00:01:36.517 --> 00:01:41.780
questions." Well, Daddy Longlegs, I never told her this, but I'll tell

27
00:01:41.781 --> 00:01:45.148
you. I've always hated the name

28
00:01:45.149 --> 00:01:50.314
Jerusha. But I like Judy. It's such a silly

29
00:01:50.315 --> 00:01:54.920
name. It belongs to someone who is the complete opposite of

30
00:01:54.921 --> 00:02:00.793
me. A sweet little blue-eyed thing, whose family adores and spoils

31
00:02:00.794 --> 00:02:05.351
her. I may have faults, but no one can say that I've been

32
00:02:05.352 --> 00:02:09.200
spoiled. So from now on, please call me

33
00:02:09.201 --> 00:02:12.528
Judy. I have some more news for

34
00:02:12.529 --> 00:02:15.752
you. I made the basketball

35
00:02:15.753 --> 00:02:19.268
team! You should see the bruise I got the other day at

36
00:02:19.269 --> 00:02:24.191
practice. My left shoulder is blue and purple with orange

37
00:02:24.192 --> 00:02:29.197
streaks. For your information, Julia Pendleton didn't make the

38
00:02:29.198 --> 00:02:33.605
team. Hooray! (I really am very mean

39
00:02:33.606 --> 00:02:37.931
sometimes.) I like college more and more every

40
00:02:37.932 --> 00:02:45.581
day. I like the girls (except for Julia) and the teachers and the classes and the

41
00:02:45.582 --> 00:02:48.426
food. We have ice cream twice a

42
00:02:48.427 --> 00:02:53.912
week. We never have cornmeal mush, which we had often at the

43
00:02:53.913 --> 00:02:56.904
orphanage. Oh, I just remembered

44
00:02:56.905 --> 00:02:59.728
something! I never told you about my

45
00:02:59.729 --> 00:03:04.486
clothes. Before school started, Miss Pritchard took me

46
00:03:04.487 --> 00:03:09.648
shopping. (She's on the ladies' committee at the orphanage, but I guess you remember that.)

47
00:03:09.649 --> 00:03:13.354
Anyway, I thought I'd get one new dress.

48
00:03:13.355 --> 00:03:17.487
Well, maybe two since you were paying for them.

49
00:03:17.488 --> 00:03:20.613
But here's what happened:

50
00:03:20.614 --> 00:03:24.379
"I like this dress for classes," I said as we shopped.

51
00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:28.618
"And I like this one for church. May I get both?"

52
00:03:28.619 --> 00:03:31.153
Miss Pritchard smiled kindly at me.

53
00:03:31.154 --> 00:03:35.109
"You'll need more than two dresses at college."

54
00:03:35.110 --> 00:03:36.800
"I will?"

55
00:03:36.801 --> 00:03:43.783
"Yes, dear. You'll need a suit and an evening dress and at least one dinner dress."

56
00:03:43.784 --> 00:03:48.296
Who knew college required so many clothes?

57
00:03:48.297 --> 00:03:56.143
So, thanks to you, I now own a dress for classes, a dress for church, and a suit.

58
00:03:56.144 --> 00:04:00.779
Plus, two dinner dresses and a silk evening dress!

59
00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:05.420
Can you imagine me—an orphan—in an evening dress?

60
00:04:05.421 --> 00:04:09.792
I also have three pairs of fine white gloves.

61
00:04:09.793 --> 00:04:13.935
At the orphanage I received mittens for Christmas.

62
00:04:13.936 --> 00:04:17.674
But I never had beautiful gloves like these.

63
00:04:17.675 --> 00:04:20.950
I like them so much that I often try them on.

64
00:04:20.951 --> 00:04:23.946
I wish I could wear them to class!

65
00:04:23.947 --> 00:04:27.687
You probably think I'm behaving like a silly girl.

66
00:04:27.688 --> 00:04:30.744
And that you're wasting your money educating me.

67
00:04:30.745 --> 00:04:37.438
But, Daddy Longlegs, if you'd worn blue gingham most of your life, you'd understand!

68
00:04:37.439 --> 00:04:41.115
When I started high school, things got even worse.

69
00:04:41.116 --> 00:04:45.971
My clothes came from the village poor box.

70
00:04:45.972 --> 00:04:51.942
On the first day of school, I sat next to a girl who used to own my dress.

71
00:04:51.943 --> 00:04:56.958
"Did you see her dress?" the girl next to me whispered to a friend.

72
00:04:56.959 --> 00:04:58.873
"Whose dress?"

73
00:04:58.874 --> 00:05:03.493
"The girl from the orphanage. Jerusha."

74
00:05:03.494 --> 00:05:06.372
The second girl studied me.

75
00:05:06.373 --> 00:05:12.048
"Didn't you have a dress like that last year?" she said to the first girl.

76
00:05:12.049 --> 00:05:19.550
"Yes! But it's out of style, so my mother put it in the poor box."

77
00:05:19.551 --> 00:05:23.243
Then they laughed while I turned red with shame.

78
00:05:23.244 --> 00:05:26.996
And this happened every year!

79
00:05:26.997 --> 00:05:30.017
I'll never forget that feeling.

80
00:05:30.018 --> 00:05:34.702
Do you see why I appreciate my new clothes?

81
00:05:34.703 --> 00:05:40.947
Six outfits—all pretty and new and bought just for me.

82
00:05:40.948 --> 00:05:45.792
One last thing: I know I shouldn't expect any letters from you.

83
00:05:45.793 --> 00:05:49.889
And Mrs. Lippett told me not to bother you with questions.

84
00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:52.985
But I just want to know a few things.

85
00:05:52.986 --> 00:05:56.545
Are you very old or just a little old?

86
00:05:56.546 --> 00:06:01.130
Are you completely bald or only a little bald?

87
00:06:01.131 --> 00:06:04.464
It's hard to picture you without some details.

88
00:06:04.465 --> 00:06:06.024
Your well-dressed student,

89
00:06:06.025 --> 00:06:09.395
Judy Abbott

